- `--dry-run` always available on any mutating subcommand; default behavior on first invocation prints a plan and exits, requiring an explicit `--apply` flag to execute (flip the default: dry-run is default, apply is opt-in)
- Tiered safety model: classify each operation as `safe` (additive, e.g. compact missing fragments), `reversible` (creates a backup before proceeding), or `destructive` (requires `--yes` + explicit named resource, e.g. `bth compact --force-rebuild --yes --target bathos.db`); different defaults per tier
- Print a repair plan as a shell script to stdout (e.g., `bth compact --force-rebuild --plan > repair.sh`), let user inspect and execute it manually; no interactive prompt path at all
- Single interactive confirmation after printing a summary diff of what will change: "3 runs will be re-ingested, 1 orphaned record will be removed. Apply? [y/N]" — `--yes` skips the prompt, `--no` (or default enter) aborts
- Backup-then-proceed: any destructive op automatically snapshots the target to `~/.bth/catalog/backups/<timestamp>_<name>` before mutating, prints the backup path, proceeds without a prompt; `--no-backup` opt-out for CI/automation
- `--dry-run` emits structured JSON (via `--output json`) or human table listing each action with `action`, `target`, `reversible` fields; a separate `bth repair apply <plan-file>` command ingests that JSON and executes it, creating an auditable apply-from-file workflow
